================================================================================
flatgraph string-pool ceiling - first-hand re-verification by this generation
harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-ceiling-reverify.log
================================================================================

================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  That the failure which prevents this run from persisting a graph over the
  complete input set is a fixed JVM array-length bound rather than a heap
  shortage, established by running the probe at two different heaps in this
  clone rather than by citing an earlier run's conclusion. flatgraph 0.1.32
  serialises the graph's entire deduplicated string pool by UTF-8 encoding
  each string and appending it to ONE java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream
  (flatgraph.storage.WriterContext.finish, Serialization.scala:174-176). A
  ByteArrayOutputStream is backed by a single contiguous byte[], and the JVM
  caps an array at Integer.MAX_VALUE - 8 = 2,147,483,639 elements.

================================================================================
WHY IT MATTERS HERE
================================================================================

  AAP 0.1.1 requires the graph to be created by this run over every JAR the
  build produced, AAP 0.3.2 forbids trimming the input set, and AAP 0.4.3
  forbids upgrading Joern. The frontend invocation over the full-reactor
  build's complete 191-archive input set hit this bound at the serialization
  phase, having already built the graph in memory:
  'java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Required array length 2147483639 + 72 is too
  large' (harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-frontend.log). Raising the heap cannot
  move a bound on one array's length, and the two arms below are the
  measurement that says so rather than an argument that it should be so.

================================================================================
THE PROBE, AND BOTH ARMS VERBATIM
================================================================================

  Source: harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-frontend-ceiling-probe.txt, retained
  verbatim by the invocation that first hit the bound. It drives
  java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream's own growth path to the soft maximum array
  length and then writes the 77 further bytes the frontend's next string would
  have written. Compiled and run in this clone with the JDK 21 every Joern JVM
  uses (Temurin 21.0.12.1+1).

$ /opt/blitzy-tools/jdk/jdk-21.0.12.1+1/bin/javac CeilingProbe.java && java -Xmx64g CeilingProbe
java.version      = 21.0.12.1
java.vm.name      = OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM
maxMemory bytes   = 68719476736
SOFT_MAX          = 2147483639
buffered bytes    = 2147483639 (== SOFT_MAX: true)
now writing 77 more bytes, exactly as the frontend's next string would
THREW            = java.lang.OutOfMemoryError
MESSAGE          = Required array length 2147483639 + 77 is too large
buffered bytes   = 2147483639

$ java -Xmx128g CeilingProbe
java.version      = 21.0.12.1
java.vm.name      = OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM
maxMemory bytes   = 137438953472
SOFT_MAX          = 2147483639
buffered bytes    = 2147483639 (== SOFT_MAX: true)
now writing 77 more bytes, exactly as the frontend's next string would
THREW            = java.lang.OutOfMemoryError
MESSAGE          = Required array length 2147483639 + 77 is too large
buffered bytes   = 2147483639

================================================================================
WHAT THE TWO ARMS SHOW
================================================================================

  Identical failure at -Xmx64g and at -Xmx128g: java.lang.OutOfMemoryError
  'Required array length 2147483639 + 77 is too large', with 2,147,483,639
  bytes already buffered in both cases. The maxMemory the JVM reports differs
  by a factor of two between the arms and the failure point does not move by
  one byte. The bound is therefore on the array, not on the heap, and it
  scales with the breadth of the input set - the total UTF-8 size of the
  graph's distinct method, type and file names - rather than with the memory
  or time spent building the graph.

================================================================================
CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY
================================================================================

  A graph over every JAR the full reactor produced cannot be persisted by the
  pinned frontend at this pin, by any heap. The AAP's own guidance for that
  situation is to stop and report rather than to substitute a narrowed graph,
  so the graph this generation's Joern stages load is named, digested and re-
  verified before every load, its provenance (written by provisioning, not by
  this run) is stated wherever it is cited, and the unmet requirement is
  published as a divergence in oss-scan-results/build-record.md and oss-scan-
  results/run-record.md. No narrowed graph is presented as compliant input,
  and no coverage conclusion is drawn from bytecode the graph does not
  contain.

